 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for committed-change delivery.** Five-second HTTPS polling would offer simpler stateless operation but requires repeated reads and introduces several seconds of lag. A hosted realtime service would reduce connection operations but adds recurring fees and provider coupling. Self-hosted WebSockets provide fast updates without a realtime vendor dependency; the project accepts responsibility for connection capacity, reconnect handling, replayable revisions, and reliable publication. See [Decision: Self-hosted WebSocket delivery](decisions/self-hosted-websocket-delivery.md).
